ce I know the Landlord is blocked, passing is an obvious choice. There's no reason to interfere with a partner in a great position.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation:\nThe current situation is critical but favorable. My teammate (Previous Player) has just played the Black Joker to beat the Landlord\'s 2. My teammate has only 2 cards remaining. I hold the Red Joker, which is the only card that can beat the Black Joker. The Landlord (Next Player) has 6 cards.\n\nIf I play the Red Joker:\n1. I waste the strongest card in the game on my own teammate.\n2. I take the lead with a very heavy hand (13 cards remaining: 333, 5, 77, 88, 1010, QQ, 2). I cannot easily clear my hand.\n3. I might prevent my teammate from playing a winning hand (e.g., if they have a pair left, they need the lead to play it).\n\nIf I pass:\n1. Since I hold the Red Joker, the Landlord strictly cannot beat the Black Joker.\n2. My teammate is guaranteed to win the trick.\n3. My teammate gets to play freely from their remaining 2 cards.\n   - If they have a pair (e.g., 44, JJ, KK), they play it and we win immediately.\n   - If they have two singles, they can play one. Even if it\'s small, I can use my Red Joker or 2 later to cover the Landlord if necessary, but letting them play their strategy is paramount when they are this close to winning.\n\nReason:\nMy teammate played the Black Joker to seize control, likely to play their final cards. Since I hold the Red Joker, I know the Landlord cannot beat the Black Joker. Beating my teammate here would be a disastrous mistake. I must let my teammate win the trick and take the lead to potentially finish the game.\n\nAnswer:\n{"cards": []}'}
